Z||000005||Fat||03-18-2001||03:37 PM||||I loved my Maguras back before they started "F"ing up on me.  But they were old and the seals got worn out.  I went to the XT Vbrakes, but they suck almost as much.  They work well, but the front one sounds like a hound caught in a barbed wire fence.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||03-19-2001||12:08 AM||||The whole problem with Shimano's parrallel(sp?) push design is they have waaay too much slop in the linkage.  I never had a problem when I was running XTR's, but XT's are notorious for having a ton of slop.  The Arch Supremes are great, fully cnc machined, 2 cartridge bearings in each arm, no slop whatsoever.  Hella expensive, though (80 bucks a side for mine, even with my 10% above cost employee discount..) which is the main reasone Avid stopped making them.  About the best v-brake out there now is the Avid Single Digit 2.0.. cheap, light, and solid.  Or Shimano's Deore V-brakes.  Sometimes you just can't beat simplicity  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>ok that was a little off-topic.  sorry 'bout that.  As far as hydraulic brakes go, they're a little heavy and a pain to set up but once all that's done they're great.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||03-19-2001||01:25 AM||||I found (if I remember correctly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> these ones were great for setting up. You undid two bolts, and then slid the pad around to where you wanted it. Since they move parallel, you don't have to worry about arch or anything.||||reg||1
